<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>BrowserAI</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>BrowserAI</AssemblyName>
    <!-- longPathAware. Session directories are the caller's choice and nothing
         constrains their depth, so a path crossing MAX_PATH must not become a
         mystery failure somewhere inside a browser profile tree. -->
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <!--
      Required by [LibraryImport]. The source generator emits unsafe marshalling
      stubs and the build fails with SYSLIB1062 without this. Not a licence to
      hand-write unsafe code: every use is a P/Invoke declaration under Interop\.

      WHY [LibraryImport] RATHER THAN [DllImport]. Corrected 2026-08-17
      (previously: "because DllImport relies on runtime IL-stub generation that
      NativeAOT does not do"). THAT REASON IS FALSE ON WINDOWS and was never
      measured. ILC compiles DllImport marshalling stubs ahead of time, exactly
      as it does LibraryImport's. Measured 2026-08-17 on SDK 10.0.400 / ILC
      10.0.11, win-x64, net10.0-windows: a probe carrying 38 [DllImport]
      declarations across kernel32, user32, ntdll and rstrtmgr, SetLastError
      true throughout and including StringBuilder marshalling and a managed
      callback delegate, published NativeAOT with ZERO trim or AOT warnings and
      returned correctly from all 41 runtime checks.

      The rule survives; only its justification was wrong. The real reasons are
      that [LibraryImport] is Microsoft's documented first recommendation for
      .NET 7 and later, that the marshalling it generates is ordinary C# that a
      human can read and step through rather than a stub the toolchain hides,
      and that SYSLIB1054 exists to move code toward it.

      Recorded at this length because a false reason is worse than no reason:
      anyone asking "could we use a generator that emits [DllImport]?" would
      have read the old sentence and concluded "impossible under AOT". It is
      not impossible, and the answer to that question has to be reached on
      other grounds. See TODO.md, "Audit the repository for justifications
      that are stated as fact and were never measured".
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Version">
    <!--
      THE VERSION IS DERIVED FROM THE NEAREST GIT TAG AND IS TYPED NOWHERE.

      MinVer reads `git describe` at build time. CITATION CORRECTED 2026-08-18
      (previously plan/stack.md, "Versions come from git tags"): that file was
      deleted with the rest of the implementation plan on 2026-08-17 and this
      pointer was left aimed at nothing. The surviving statement of the same
      decision is STACK.md, "Versions come from git tags"; the measured
      behaviour is kb/packaging/velopack.md.

        on the tag v0.1.0                  = 0.1.0
        five commits later, no new tag     = 0.1.1 with an alpha.0.5 suffix
        no tag reachable at all            = 0.0.0 with an alpha.0.N suffix

      The prefix is `v`, which is the house prefix across the repositories this
      one shares conventions with. It is a tag prefix rather than a version, so
      it is not a pin and carries no digits.

      On the PRODUCT PROJECT ONLY. Versioning the test project buys nothing: no
      artifact is cut from it and no caller ever sees its version.
    -->
    <MinVerTagPrefix>v</MinVerTagPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Native compilation">
    <!--
      AOT IS GATED TO PUBLISH, and this single property is what does it:
      `dotnet build` never invokes ILC, `dotnet publish` does. Everyday builds
      stay fast; only a release pays for native compilation and the MSVC link.

      PREREQUISITE, recorded here because its absence presents as the wrong
      diagnosis: ILC's final step is a native link, so a publish needs the MSVC
      toolchain (link.exe, located through vswhere, installed by Visual
      Studio's "Desktop development with C++" workload). Without it the publish
      fails in a way that reads as an SDK or library incompatibility, and that
      wrong diagnosis leads straight to the wrong fix. A shipped product
      settled for self-contained single-file instead of AOT for exactly this
      reason, and only worked out why afterwards.

      Set unconditionally rather than under a publish-only condition: the
      trimming feature switches key off this property, so conditioning it would
      make `dotnet build` and `dotnet publish` behave differently.
    -->
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>

    <!-- Analyse on every build, not only at publish. PublishAot already turns
         these on; they are named so the guarantee survives someone toggling
         PublishAot, and so that what is enforced is readable here. -->
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>

    <!--
      ILC OUTPUT MUST BE EMPTY. A trim or AOT warning fails the publish; it is
      a silent-failure warrant, not advice.

      WHICH PROPERTY ACTUALLY DOES THAT, corrected 2026-08-17 (previously the
      three properties below were described as though ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors
      were the one that fails the publish). IT IS NOT. The property that
      promotes an ILC warning to an error is TreatWarningsAsErrors, set
      repository wide in Directory.Build.props.

      Measured 2026-08-17, SDK 10.0.400 / ILC 10.0.11, win-x64, on a probe
      emitting 14 IL2xxx/IL3xxx diagnostics, five variants:

        ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors alone   exit 0, 14 warnings, binary produced
        TreatWarningsAsErrors alone         exit 1, 14 errors,   MSB3077, no binary
        both                                exit 1, 14 errors,   MSB3077, no binary
        neither                             exit 0, 14 warnings, binary produced
        ILLink true + TreatWarnings false   exit 0, 14 warnings, binary produced

      So ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors had NO observable effect at all here: rows
      1, 4 and 5 are identical. TreatWarningsAsErrors alone is both necessary
      and sufficient.

      THE CURRENT CONFIGURATION IS SAFE AND NOTHING NEEDS CHANGING.
      Directory.Build.props sets TreatWarningsAsErrors true for every project,
      so the publish does fail on ILC warnings today. The property below is kept
      rather than deleted: it is asserted by BuildConfigurationTests as one of
      the per-assembly-only knobs, it is harmless, and it is the documented knob
      for this intent even though this toolchain does not appear to honour it on
      its own. What is corrected is the ATTRIBUTION, because someone tuning
      either property later would otherwise draw the wrong conclusion, and
      removing TreatWarningsAsErrors while trusting the property below would
      silently reopen the exact hole this block exists to close.

      TrimmerSingleWarn=false is what makes the report name the member rather
      than emitting one line per assembly.
    -->
    <SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>false</SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" />
    <PackageReference Include="Velopack" />
    <!-- Build-time only. PrivateAssets="all" keeps it out of the dependency
         graph entirely, so nothing ships and ILC never sees it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="MinVer" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The product's own bans. The analyzer itself, and the repository-wide
         never-by-image-name file it reads alongside this one, are declared in
         Directory.Build.props so that every project is covered. -->
    <AdditionalFiles Include="BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The suite asserts on internals by design: the point of the app-paths
         seam and the stdio wrapper is that they are testable, and exporting
         them publicly to achieve that would be a worse trade. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="BrowserAI.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="BrowserAI.TestProbe" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE PAYLOAD SHIPS BESIDE THE BINARY, AND ONLY ON PUBLISH.

    PayloadLayout resolves node.exe and cli.js from <binary>\payload, which is
    what the installed artifact looks like: the payload is a directory tree
    inside current\, replaced wholesale by each update, which is what keeps
    Velopack's per-file delta cheap.

    Publish-only, for the same reason native compilation is: `dotnet build` must
    stay fast and must not copy ~107 MB. Build-Payload.ps1 assembles the tree
    and is not wired into either. On a clean clone there is no payload/, the
    condition below is false, and the publish still succeeds, which build-order
    step 1's done-test requires.

    (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment: one makes MSBuild fail to load
    the file, and `dotnet build` then reports NETSDK1207 from somewhere else
    entirely. Measured twice on 2026-08-16, and hit again writing this target.)

    ResolvedFileToPublish rather than Content: Content copies on build as well,
    and there is no way to say "publish but not build" for it that survives the
    single-file and AOT publish paths.
  -->
  <!--
    A BUILD THAT DOES NOT KNOW WHAT IT IS MUST NOT PRODUCE A BINARY.

    `0.0.0` is what MinVer emits when no tag is reachable: an unfetched tag, a
    shallow clone, a tarball with no `.git` at all. Nothing about that build is
    wrong until it ships, and then it is unrollbackable, unbisectable, and
    indistinguishable in a feed from every other build that also found no tag.
    CITATION CORRECTED 2026-08-18 (previously "plan/pre-release.md item 9
    refuses it"): that file went with the implementation plan on 2026-08-17.
    The surviving checklist is RELEASING.md; this target is that refusal moved
    from a checklist a human reads to the build that produces the artifact,
    which is why deleting the checklist item did not delete the guarantee.

    It fails the BUILD rather than the publish deliberately. A check that only
    fired at publish would leave every test in this suite running against a
    binary whose version nobody had established.

    The remedy is in the message because the cause is never local: the fix for
    a CI checkout is `fetch-depth: 0`, and the fix for a hand-made clone is a
    tag fetch. Neither is guessable from the number 0.0.0.

    (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment. One makes MSBuild fail to load
    the file, and the error names a different project entirely. Measured three
    times before today and hit a fourth time writing this target, on the very
    flag the message below names.)
  -->
  <Target Name="RefuseAVersionDerivedFromNoTag" AfterTargets="MinVer">
    <Error Condition="'$(MinVerVersion)' == ''"
           Text="MinVer produced no version at all, so this build cannot say what it is. Check that the MinVer package restored and that this project is inside a git working tree." />
    <Error Condition="$(MinVerVersion.StartsWith('0.0.0'))"
           Text="This build derived the version $(MinVerVersion) from git, and a version beginning 0.0.0 means MinVer found no '$(MinVerTagPrefix)*' tag to count from. A binary that does not know what it is cannot be rolled back to or bisected against, so the build is refused rather than stamped. Fetch the tags: 'git fetch --tags', or on a CI checkout set fetch-depth to 0 (a shallow clone carries no tags and fails exactly here)." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    THE NOTICES SHIP WITH THE BINARY, AND THIS IS THE ONLY THING THAT PUTS THEM
    THERE.

    Four redistribution obligations attach at first installer handoff.
    CITATION CORRECTED 2026-08-18 (previously "plan/pre-release.md item 13"),
    deleted with the plan on 2026-08-17; the surviving statement is
    RELEASING.md and kb/packaging/dependencies.md, which carries the licence
    texts themselves. Three of them travel by themselves: node's
    LICENSE and the two Playwright packages' notices are files inside the
    payload tree, which the target below copies whole. The fourth does not.
    Velopack is a NuGet dependency compiled INTO BrowserAI.exe, so its licence
    stays in the package cache and nothing ever copies it into the artifact,
    and MIT requires the notice to travel with the binary. The trademark
    disclaimer has no upstream file at all.

    Found 2026-08-16 by the first run of the pre-release checklist, reading the
    packed .nupkg rather than the source tree: both were absent from a package
    that was otherwise releasable, and no test looked.

    Unconditional, unlike the payload: a publish with no payload/ assembled is
    a real state that must still succeed, but a publish with no notices in it
    is never one.
  -->
  <Target Name="AddNoticesToPublish" AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt">
        <RelativePath>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt</RelativePath>
        <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      </ResolvedFileToPublish>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <Target Name="AddPayloadToPublish"
          AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\payload\payload.json')">
    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        ⚠️ .cache IS EXCLUDED, AND IT WAS SHIPPING. Found 2026-08-16 at
        build-order step 19, on the first real `vpk pack`: Build-Payload.ps1
        keeps the downloaded Node archive at payload\.cache\node-<ver>-win-x64.zip
        so a re-run does not re-download it, and the unfiltered glob below put
        all 37,304,352 bytes of it into the published artifact. That is 31% of
        the payload, shipped to every machine, for a file nothing reads at
        runtime, and it compresses badly because it is already compressed. It
        was invisible because every other signal was green: the publish
        succeeded, the tests passed, and the only symptom was a number nobody
        had measured yet.
      -->
      <PayloadFile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\payload\**\*"
                   Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\payload\.cache\**\*" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Include="@(PayloadFile)">
        <RelativePath>payload\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</RelativePath>
        <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      </ResolvedFileToPublish>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
